WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.000 --> 00:00:16.000
D Chief: Morning, everyone. Roles today: presenter, scribe, first pass, second pass. Our objective is to distinguish septic arthritis from transient synovitis in a febrile toddler.

2
00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Will Barlow: Three-year-old, previously healthy, two days of fever, and since yesterday refusing to bear weight on the right leg. On the table he is holding the hip flexed and externally rotated.

3
00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:25.000
Will Barlow: Fever to 38.6 at home, no trauma anyone witnessed. Eating less. On exam he is uncomfortable, will not weight-bear at all, and resists internal rotation.

4
00:04:10.000 --> 00:04:22.000
Nadia Haddad: I have the header in and the problem representation line ready.

5
00:05:00.000 --> 00:05:14.000
Will Barlow: No rash, no other joints involved, no recent viral illness that the family recalls.

6
00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:32.000
A Resident: Three-year-old, previously healthy, two days of fever and refusal to bear weight, holding the hip flexed and externally rotated.

7
00:07:35.000 --> 00:08:01.000
A Resident: Septic arthritis of the hip is my leader, because the positioning plus the fever is what worries me. Transient synovitis is my main competitor and I cannot separate them on exam. Proximal femoral osteomyelitis is possible given the fever.

8
00:08:08.000 --> 00:08:26.000
A Resident: My can't-miss is septic arthritis, because of the joint destruction clock. What I most want is the hip ultrasound.

9
00:08:20.000 --> 00:08:30.000
Will Barlow: Yes, that is correct, it was septic arthritis of the hip.

10
00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:24.000
B Resident: Let me take this by layer, because that is what the imaging will sort out. Skin and soft tissue, then joint, then bone, then referred.

11
00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:48.000
B Resident: Skin and soft tissue: no cellulitis, off. Joint: septic arthritis and transient synovitis, both live. Bone: proximal femoral osteomyelitis, live.

12
00:11:55.000 --> 00:12:15.000
B Resident: Transient synovitis I am striking. Classic transient synovitis follows a viral illness, the child is afebrile or low grade and will usually weight-bear if you coax them. He is 38.6 and will not bear weight at all. That argues against it.

13
00:12:25.000 --> 00:12:45.000
B Resident: The discriminating test is a hip ultrasound with aspiration if there is an effusion. Effusion plus that presentation and I am calling ortho tonight. No effusion pushes me to MRI, not back to synovitis.

14
00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:30.000
B Resident: Septic arthritis, about 70 percent. What would change my mind is a dry tap with a normal-looking joint, and then I am in bone rather than joint. I am watching myself for premature closure.

15
00:16:05.000 --> 00:16:19.000
Will Barlow: Labs: CRP 8.4, ESR 62, white count 17.3. Ultrasound shows a hip effusion.

16
00:16:40.000 --> 00:16:52.000
Nadia Haddad: Striking transient synovitis in red with the reason beside it.

17
00:19:10.000 --> 00:19:26.000
B Resident: Plan is aspiration tonight, ortho on the phone now, empiric cefazolin after cultures.

18
00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:38.000
C Attending: One pearl. The Kocher criteria were derived and validated in a population with a much higher pre-test probability than a general clinic, so three of four does not carry the same weight everywhere.

19
00:22:05.000 --> 00:22:27.000
Will Barlow: It was septic arthritis of the hip. What we came to late was osteomyelitis. We anchored on the joint from the positioning, and nobody asked for the MRI until after the aspirate was equivocal. That is the miss, and it is why the smear never got looked at either.

20
00:24:05.000 --> 00:24:19.000
D Chief: Two points to take away: positioning is a strong prior but not a test, and a stated trigger is what stops premature closure. Board is exported.
